# ARCHITECTURE STANDARD

The SDD describes how the system is structured.

Before making architectural changes:
1. Inspect the current architecture.
2. Identify affected boundaries.
3. Identify dependencies.
4. Explain trade-offs.
5. Prefer the simplest architecture that satisfies requirements.

The SDD may describe:
- application layers;
- modules;
- components;
- data flows;
- APIs;
- authentication;
- authorization;
- integrations;
- persistence;
- queues;
- caching;
- storage;
- observability;
- deployment boundaries.

## ARCHITECTURAL PRINCIPLES

- Keep business logic out of presentation when the project architecture separates concerns.
- Keep domain/business rules centralized.
- Avoid circular dependencies.
- Avoid unnecessary abstractions.
- Avoid premature microservices.
- Prefer clear module boundaries.
- Preserve backward compatibility when required.
- Treat security boundaries explicitly.

Never introduce a new architectural pattern solely because it is fashionable.
